\section{Reduce the Saturated Prefix of an Application Spine}

In a graph reducer, excess arguments have already been attached to the application spine when the head redex is located. The implementation must distinguish the application node at which the combinator becomes saturated from the outermost node representing the whole demand. The following step takes a root in a well-formed graph of applications, positive-arity combinator heads, and literals. Assume that following indirections and walking the demanded left spine here both terminate; cyclic-demand handling is a separate mechanism. The step either recognizes weak head normal form, reports a nonfunction use in an untyped graph, or performs one graph contraction without dropping the remaining arguments.

\begin{verbatim}
head_step(root):
  spine = empty stack
  p = chase_indirections(root)
  while p is App(f, x):
    push (p, x) onto spine
    p = chase_indirections(f)
  if p is a literal:
    if spine is empty: return WHNF
    return NotCallable
  n = arity(p)
  if size(spine) < n: return WHNF
  # Pops recover operands in source order.
  used = pop n entries from spine
  r = application_node(last(used))
  # Overwrite the prefix, not the whole demand.
  r := Indirection(contract(p, operands(used)))
  return Reduced
\end{verbatim}

For \(((K\,I)\,99)\,7\), the outer-to-inner walk pushes entries for \(7,99,I\), in that order. The \(K\) contraction uses \(I\) and \(99\), and replaces only the node for \((K\,I)\,99\) by an indirection to \(I\). The outer application still passes \(7\); a second contraction yields the literal \(7\). Overwriting the outer root at the first step would stop at \(I\) instead. With input \(K\,I\) there is one operand for an arity-two head, so the step reports weak head normal form with zero contractions.

The spine entries identify precisely the left-nested applications of the discovered head. Updating the saturated prefix also lets another edge pointing to that prefix observe the contraction; it was never necessary to duplicate its arguments. Starting over from the outer root after each contraction is simple but can revisit a long pending spine. A production evaluator can keep the unconsumed portion on its stack across contractions, while preserving the same operand order and update address.
